//! What was left over, read the same way.
//!
//! 1262 routines, and this table is a remainder rather than a shape. Every other
//! table of this kind was built by asking for routines of one form; these are
//! simply the ones the walk could still read once those had been taken out, so
//! they have nothing in common except that. Recording them as a remainder is
//! more honest than inventing a family for them.
//!
//! What is in it is accordingly mixed. 937 of the 1262 ask no question at all —
//! a straight run of calls, too long for the tables that collect those. At the
//! other end sits the deepest routine anywhere in the program: 35 branches
//! inside one another, and the largest at 87 steps.
//!
//! That deepest one is worth naming, because what it is doing is not
//! complicated. It asks [`crate::delphi_runtime::is_of_class`] against one
//! class constant after another, each test nested inside the last one's
//! failure — a Delphi `is` cascade, thirty-five classes long, written as a
//! staircase because that is what `else if` compiles to when the compiler
//! cannot make a table of it. 5 routines here ask that question three or more
//! times. Depth in this program is very often not complexity but a list.
//!
//! 19 of them are constructors, halves in different steps in every one.

// The addresses are written without separators so the port-mapping importer can
// match them against the recovered functions.
#![allow(clippy::unreadable_literal)]

